ate, no failed services, no unnecessary listening sockets, and no weakened security controls were observed in the after_executor lifecycle snapshot versus after_prepare; temp-file and socket differences are limited to expected DHCP lease renewal and reboot log artifacts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45859ff545244ff0","cmd-c444546600d94933","cmd-a066ab2605434c64","cmd-2174beb1e0a84e33","global:before_prepare:node2:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes were satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node1's vtnet2 was correctly identified and stood up as a new 'optlanb' zone (10.60.60.1/24) with a supporting firewall rule that did not disturb existing SSH access; node3 reaches node1 directly on lan-b; and node3 reaches node2 through node1's new zone via added routes, confirmed by TTL decrement and pf state-table evidence of actual L3 forwarding rather than a flat shortcut. The independent provider-wide restart protocol was executed and evidenced for all three nodes, each showing a pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged restart, a changed boot identity, and recovered zone/route/connectivity state, with node3's reboot acknowledgment being the only slightly ambiguous step (though corroborated by subsequent timeouts and a changed boot_id). Hygiene is good; two small, bounded troubleshooting mutations (temporary interface up and a temporary node2 eth1 address) were used for interface discovery and fully reverted before task completion.","limitations":["node3's reboot command returned 'exec stream closed before command completion' rather than a clean acknowledgment, so the restart trigger itself is inferred from subsequent connection timeouts and the changed boot_id rather than a direct success confirmation.","One diagnostic tcpdump command (cmd-4f9e1b30993d4509) timed out without producing output; this did not appear to affect the final outcome since the capture was retried successfully afterward."]},"ttft